8$ is a natural number, so it belongs to $N.$ \n\nConsequently, $\\frac{72}{9}$ also belongs to ${Q}^{+}$ and $R.$\n\nSince $\\frac{72}{9}$ is positive, it does not belong to ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-37313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67ef938e9bb2f2df040845324fbacd55e6190b901c2c4d191d2f829a4d7af248"} {"question" "q-37313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-37313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, III, and IV only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-37313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, III, and IV only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-36248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "To which of the following sets do all the positive integers belong?\n\n- $N$\n- ${R}^{+}$\n- ${Q}^{-}$\n- ${Z}^{-}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-36248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The positive integer numbers are $1,2,3,…$\n\n- Since all positive integer numbers are natural numbers, all the positive integers belong to $N.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Since all positive integer numbers are positive real numbers, all the positive integer numbers belong to ${R}^{+}.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The set ${Q}^{-}$ contains all the negative rational numbers. However, positive integer numbers as, for instance, $2$ don't belong to ${Q}^{-},$ so ${Q}^{-}$ does *not* contain the positive integer numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\n- The set ${Z}^{-}$ contains all the negative integer numbers. However, positive integer numbers as, for instance, $2$ don't belong to ${Z}^{-},$ so ${Z}^{-}$ does *not* contain the positive integer numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-36248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67ef938e9bb2f2df040845324fbacd55e6190b901c2c4d191d2f829a4d7af248"} {"question" "q-36248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-36248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "III and IV only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-36248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-181544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "To which of the following sets does the number $-\\pi $ belong?\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$\n- $R$\n- $Q$\n- $C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-181544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The number $-\\pi $ is not a rational number. Consequently, $-\\pi $ does not belong to any of the sets  ${Q}^{-}$ nor $Q.$\n\nHowever, $-\\pi $ is a real number, so it belongs to $R.$\n\nFinally, since every real number is also a complex number, $-\\pi $ also belongs to $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-181544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67ef938e9bb2f2df040845324fbacd55e6190b901c2c4d191d2f829a4d7af248"} {"question" "q-181544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-181544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "q-181544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and IV only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/state.json" "file_sha256" "477ec89755d9a29cde3cf90c26ec1d1502fa5bb92159425b7250d2da55ba7332"} {"question" "e-13138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb N, \\quad \\mathbb Z, \\quad \\mathbb Q, \\quad \\mathbb R, \\quad \\mathbb C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $3.25$ belong?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/example-13138.json" "file_sha256" "19200912422a2262af1032a986df26de1fd71e0ec26215628b460b7bebf67ed3"} {"question" "e-13138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/assets/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following: \n\n- The number $3.25$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Z}$ nor $\\mathbb{N},$  so we can write\n\n$$\n3.25\\notin\\mathbb N, \\qquad 3.25\\notin\\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- However, notice that $3.25$ can be written as the ratio of two integers as follows:\n\n$$\n3.25=\\dfrac{325}{100}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $3.25$ is a rational number, and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{Q}.\n$$\n\n- Every rational is a real and complex number. Consequently, $3.25$ belongs to both $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb{C},$ and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad 3.25 \\in \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbb Q, \\mathbb R,$ and $\\mathbb C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/example-13138.json" "file_sha256" "19200912422a2262af1032a986df26de1fd71e0ec26215628b460b7bebf67ed3"} {"question" "e-13139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-?$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$\n\n- $- 0.2$\n\n- $0$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/example-13139.json" "file_sha256" "49ac1fb4b106879d37bc35b91c21524f5f3381e3b4d867cc2d709c41946a267c"} {"question" "e-13139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative but not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $-0.2$ is negative, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n-0.2 = -\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-0.2$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $0$ is a rational number but not negative, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4} =- 2$, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt 4 = -2 = -\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\sqrt 4$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/example-13139.json" "file_sha256" "49ac1fb4b106879d37bc35b91c21524f5f3381e3b4d867cc2d709c41946a267c"} {"question" "e-13140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "To which of the following sets do all the negative integers belong? \n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}^-$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/example-13140.json" "file_sha256" "b122e1a29b5876fee1d245b506e08f7db4de5e8373b82e0d5f938a88d7185d89"} {"question" "e-13140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The negative integers are $-1, -2, -3, \\ldots$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ contains all the integers, including the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ contains the positive integers and $0.$ So, it does not contain the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative rational numbers. Since all integers are rational numbers, $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative real numbers and $0.$ Since all integers are real numbers, $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/example-13140.json" "file_sha256" "b122e1a29b5876fee1d245b506e08f7db4de5e8373b82e0d5f938a88d7185d89"} {"question" "q-80234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Z,Q,R,$ and $C$ only"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "reference/ma