c79578b6aad97e90bd8de29bb31c712b" "rationale" "The number -5 is an integer, hence rational (-5/1), real, and complex (-5+0i). It is negative and therefore not natural. Exactly Z, Q, R, and C contain it, making original option 1 correct. The stored '$Z, Q, R$, and $C$ only' has identical meaning. This is a source-backed derived judgment, corroborated by the saved worked solution and successful grading; no contradictory answer key exists."}] "usage" {"e-13140" [] "e-13139" [] "q-181441" [] "q-80234" [] "q-80336" [] "e-13138" []} "basis" 1332 "retractions" [[17592186054986 :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$N, Z, Q, R, C$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\sqrt{-1}$ belong?"] [17592186054986 :question/answer-fields 17592186051839] [17592186055409 :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$N, Z, Q, R, C$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $-5$ belong?"] [17592186055409 :question/answer-fields 17592186054126] [17592186055412 :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to $R_{0}^{-}$?\n\n1. $- π$\n2. $\\sqrt{3}$\n3. $-\\frac{4}{5}$\n4. $0$"] [17592186054342 :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n$\\mathbb N, \\quad \\mathbb Z, \\quad \\mathbb Q, \\quad \\mathbb R, \\quad \\mathbb C$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $3.25$ belong?"] [17592186054342 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/47/Source/Images/0d72f30929eeba5e9f3763f164935f47.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents the set of **integers**.\n- $\\mathbb{Q}$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n- $\\mathbb{R}$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n- $\\mathbb C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $3.25$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Z}$ nor $\\mathbb{N}$, so we can write\n$3.25\\notin\\mathbb N, \\qquad 3.25\\notin\\mathbb Z$.\n- However, notice that $3.25$ can be written as the ratio of two integers as follows:\n$3.25=\\dfrac{325}{100}$\nConsequently, $3.25$ is a rational number, and we can write\n$3.25 \\in \\mathbb{Q}$.\n- Every rational is a real and complex number. Consequently, $3.25$ belongs to both $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb{C}$, and we can write\n$3.25 \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad 3.25 \\in \\mathbb{C}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbb Q, \\mathbb R$, and $\\mathbb C$ only.\""] [17592186054343 :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-$?\n\n1. $-\\sqrt{3}$\n2. $-0.2$\n3. $0$\n4. $-\\sqrt{4}$"] [17592186054343 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative but not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $-0.2$ is negative, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n$-0.2 = -\\dfrac{1}{5}$\nTherefore, $-0.2$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- $0$ is a rational number but not negative, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $-\\sqrt{4} =-2$, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n$-\\sqrt 4 = -2 = -\\dfrac{2}{1}$\nTherefore, $-\\sqrt 4$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""] [17592186054344 :question/problem "To which of the following sets do all the negative integers belong?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{Z}$\n2. $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$\n3. $\\mathbb{Q}^-$\n4. $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$"] [17592186054344 :question/worked-solution "The negative integers are $-1, -2, -3, \\ldots$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ contains all the integers, including the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ contains the positive integers and $0$. So, it does not contain the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative rational numbers. Since all integers are rational numbers, $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative real numbers and $0$. Since all integers are real numbers, $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186051840 17592186051841 17592186051842 17592186051843 17592186051844 17592186054127 17592186054128 17592186054129 17592186054130 17592186054131 17592186054145 17592186054146 17592186054147 17592186054148 17592186054149] "immutable_fields" [[17592186051839 :answer-field/correct] [17592186051839 :answer-field/id] [17592186051839 :answer-field/key] [17592186051839 :answer-field/type] [17592186054126 :answer-field/correct] [17592186054126 :answer-field/id] [17592186054126 :answer-field/key] [17592186054126 :answer-field/type] [17592186054144 :answer-field/correct] [17592186054144 :answer-field/id] [17592186054144 :answer-field/key] [17592186054144 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
